    <edge source="programming_record:programming_data_csv:3" target="mstc_challenge:increasing_experimental_capacity_at_nuclear_rese:e9a4a1153b" id="classified_as_challenge:programming_record_programming_data_csv_3">
      <data key="d11">classified_as_challenge</data>
      <data key="d12">1</data>
      <data key="d13">classification_pipeline</data>
      <data key="d14">3</data>
      <data key="d15">medium</data>
      <data key="d16">False</data>
      <data key="d17">The scope focuses on productionizing AI tools for weapons design, modeling, and simulation workflows (CHANDLER federated model, Horizon Shield simulation workflow, CADSAT meshing tool) and deploying supporting infrastructure and a Secure AI suite on classified networks. These are generic AI/ML and simulation-enabling capabilities rather than facility operations or explicit DA/PA integration. Among the three options, they align best with the data- and simulation-readiness aspects of “Harnessing America’s Historic Nuclear Data and Research,” which emphasizes AI pipelines, meshing, and converting information into simulation-ready datasets. There is no clear evidence of an AI facility operating system or DA/PA production integration, so the other two challenges fit less well. Confidence is medium because the description is broad and could support multiple Genesis efforts. SME correction: Corrected</data>
      <data key="d18">UFR</data>
      <data key="d19">346644000.0</data>
      <data key="d20">NA11</data>
    </edge>
