      <data key="d15">low</data>
      <data key="d16">True</data>
      <data key="d17">The scope description is very high-level, stating that this UFR is a significant change from a prior NA-11 UFR titled “Developing Artificial Intelligence Tools for Nuclear Deterrence” and that it supports the newly launched Genesis Mission and development of AI capabilities. There is no explicit mention of business/management processes or acquisition systems, which makes the two more enterprise-focused challenges a weaker fit. The nuclear deterrence emphasis, combined with the WBS and STAT context in Stockpile Research and HED &amp; Ignition Science for Stockpile Applications, most closely aligns with AI applied to weapons design/production and deterrence, which is the focus of “Integrating Design and Production Operations for Nuclear Deterrence.” However, because the description does not explicitly reference design–production integration or DA/PA workflows, the mapping is uncertain, leading to a low confidence rating.</data>
      <data key="d18">UFR</data>
      <data key="d19">20400000.0</data>
      <data key="d20">NA11</data>
    </edge>
