    <edge source="sub_office:na90:e748b0fc5d" target="mstc_challenge:streamlining_production_removing_red_tape_and_en:5c22e0a6a3" id="funds_challenge_evidence:programming_record_programming_data_fynsp27_wide_csv_56">
      <data key="d11">funds_challenge_evidence</data>
      <data key="d12">1</data>
      <data key="d13">classification_pipeline</data>
      <data key="d14">56</data>
      <data key="d15">medium</data>
      <data key="d16">True</data>
      <data key="d17">The scope describes an enterprise AI tool to inform NNSA infrastructure decision making and help NNSA “Build SMART with confidence” within Maintenance and Repair of Facilities. This aligns more with streamlining facility and infrastructure planning, documentation, and regulatory/safety-related decision processes than with pure acquisition workflows or materials R&amp;D. Among the options, the challenge focused on streamlining production and regulatory/safety processes in high-hazard facilities best matches an AI decision-support tool for infrastructure and operations, even though the description is more about planning than explicit safety-basis automation, so confidence is medium.</data>
      <data key="d18">UFR</data>
      <data key="d19">32950000.0</data>
      <data key="d20">NA90</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="sub_office:na90:e748b0fc5d" target="mstc_challenge:increasing_experimental_capacity_at_nuclear_rese:e9a4a1153b" id="funds_challenge_evidence:programming_record_programming_data_csv_48">
      <data key="d11">funds_challenge_evidence</data>
      <data key="d12">1</data>
      <data key="d13">classification_pipeline</data>
      <data key="d14">48</data>
      <data key="d15">low</data>
      <data key="d16">True</data>
      <data key="d17">The scope describes replacing overloaded telecommunications towers to meet current and future communication demands, which is generic infrastructure/operations support. Among the three options, improved communications infrastructure most plausibly supports increased experimental capacity at facilities (reliable data/communications for experiments) rather than directly streamlining regulatory processes or accelerating materials R&amp;D. However, the mapping is indirect and not explicit, so confidence is low.</data>
      <data key="d18">UFR</data>
      <data key="d19">46400000.0</data>
      <data key="d20">NA90</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="sub_office:na90:e748b0fc5d" target="mstc_challenge:increasing_experimental_capacity_at_nuclear_rese:e9a4a1153b" id="funds_challenge_evidence:programming_record_programming_data_csv_49">
      <data key="d11">funds_challenge_evidence</data>
      <data key="d12">1</data>
      <data key="d13">classification_pipeline</data>
      <data key="d14">49</data>
      <data key="d15">medium</data>
      <data key="d16">True</data>
      <data key="d17">The scope is to increase electrical import and distribution capacity to LANL, including new lines to the Supercomputing Center and between substations. This is mission-enabling infrastructure that directly supports higher experimental and computational throughput at a nuclear research site, aligning best with increasing experimental capacity at nuclear research facilities. It does not focus on acquisition process optimization or materials discovery/qualification, so the acquisition and materials challenges are less applicable.</data>
      <data key="d18">BASELINE</data>
      <data key="d19">115104000.0</data>
      <data key="d20">NA90</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="sub_office:na90:e748b0fc5d" target="mstc_challenge:increasing_experimental_capacity_at_nuclear_rese:e9a4a1153b" id="funds_challenge_evidence:programming_record_programming_data_csv_50">
      <data key="d11">funds_challenge_evidence</data>
      <data key="d12">1</data>
      <data key="d13">classification_pipeline</data>
      <data key="d14">50</data>
      <data key="d15">low</data>
      <data key="d16">False</data>
      <data key="d17">The scope is construction of a new central computing facility to ensure reliable classified and unclassified computing availability and house network/data servers. This is generic mission-enabling compute infrastructure rather than clearly tied to materials workflows or DA/PA design-production integration. Among the options, it most plausibly supports the data- and compute-intensive needs of digitizing, storing, and serving historic nuclear data, but the mapping is indirect, so confidence is low. SME correction: Corrected</data>
      <data key="d18">BASELINE</data>
      <data key="d19">183700000.0</data>
      <data key="d20">NA90</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="sub_office:na90:e748b0fc5d" target="mstc_challenge:increasing_experimental_capacity_at_nuclear_rese:e9a4a1153b" id="funds_challenge_evidence:programming_record_programming_data_csv_52">
      <data key="d11">funds_challenge_evidence</data>
      <data key="d12">1</data>
      <data key="d13">classification_pipeline</data>
      <data key="d14">52</data>
      <data key="d15">low</data>
      <data key="d16">True</data>
      <data key="d17">The scope description only indicates an electrical power capacity upgrade at LANL, which most directly supports physical infrastructure and potentially experimental facility capacity. Among the options, this aligns best with Increasing Experimental Capacity at Nuclear Research Facilities, as added electrical capacity is a prerequisite for higher experimental throughput. However, the evidence does not explicitly reference experiments, facilities, or acquisition/business processes, so the mapping is uncertain.</data>
      <data key="d18">BASELINE</data>
      <data key="d19">2824000.0</data>
      <data key="d20">NA90</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="sub_office:na90:e748b0fc5d" target="mstc_challenge:increasing_experimental_capacity_at_nuclear_rese:e9a4a1153b" id="funds_challenge_evidence:programming_record_programming_data_csv_53">
      <data key="d11">funds_challenge_evidence</data>
      <data key="d12">1</data>
      <data key="d13">classification_pipeline</data>
      <data key="d14">53</data>
      <data key="d15">low</data>
      <data key="d16">True</data>
      <data key="d17">The scope description only indicates funding for a “New Central Computing Facility, Y-12 OPCs,” which is infrastructure for computing rather than clearly materials-focused or acquisition-focused work. Among the three options, expanded computing capacity most plausibly supports increased experimental and simulation throughput at a nuclear site, aligning best with the experimental capacity challenge, but the evidence is minimal and indirect, so confidence is low.</data>
      <data key="d18">BASELINE</data>
      <data key="d19">3399000.0</data>
      <data key="d20">NA90</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="sub_office:na90:e748b0fc5d" target="mstc_challenge:accelerating_business_and_management_processes_f:1739dbeef6" id="funds_challenge_evidence:programming_record_programming_data_csv_51">
      <data key="d11">funds_challenge_evidence</data>
      <data key="d12">1</data>
      <data key="d13">classification_pipeline</data>
      <data key="d14">51</data>
      <data key="d15">medium</data>
      <data key="d16">True</data>
      <data key="d17">The scope description, “Telecommunication &amp; AI Infrastructure Program Integration,” and the interest area “AI GENESIS PLATFORM” indicate an effort to build and maintain shared AI and IT infrastructure/platform capabilities rather than specific nuclear experimental facilities or materials R&amp;D. This aligns best with the business/management challenge, which emphasizes a unified platform and AI tools to integrate and accelerate enterprise workflows and data, matching the idea of an AI infrastructure platform supporting broader mission operations. The facilities and materials-focused challenges are less consistent with the generic platform and operations-of-facilities context provided.</data>
      <data key="d18">UFR</data>
      <data key="d19">15220000.0</data>
      <data key="d20">NA90</data>
    </edge>
