    <edge source="sub_office:naim:e600e447ad" target="mstc_challenge:accelerating_business_and_management_processes_f:1739dbeef6" id="funds_challenge_evidence:programming_record_programming_data_csv_58">
      <data key="d11">funds_challenge_evidence</data>
      <data key="d12">1</data>
      <data key="d13">classification_pipeline</data>
      <data key="d14">58</data>
      <data key="d15">medium</data>
      <data key="d16">True</data>
      <data key="d17">The scope description is generic—funding annual licenses and tokens for AI—but the program interest area is explicitly the AI GENESIS PLATFORM, which aligns best with an enterprise-wide AI ecosystem supporting business and management workflows. There is no indication of a specific focus on acquisition (AMIS) or historic nuclear data digitization; instead, this appears to fund core AI platform capabilities that underpin broader business and management process acceleration, matching the third challenge’s emphasis on an integrated AI platform for PPBE and other management functions.</data>
      <data key="d18">BASELINE</data>
      <data key="d19">2500000.0</data>
      <data key="d20">NAIM</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="sub_office:naim:e600e447ad" target="mstc_challenge:accelerating_business_and_management_processes_f:1739dbeef6" id="funds_challenge_evidence:programming_record_programming_data_csv_59">
      <data key="d11">funds_challenge_evidence</data>
      <data key="d12">1</data>
      <data key="d13">classification_pipeline</data>
      <data key="d14">59</data>
      <data key="d15">medium</data>
      <data key="d16">False</data>
      <data key="d17">The scope description is broad—funding AI investments in general—but the program interest area is explicitly the AI GENESIS PLATFORM, which aligns most closely with the business and management–oriented Genesis platform described in the “Accelerating Business and Management Processes for National Security Missions” challenge. The STAT codes (Information Technology &amp; Cybersecurity) and WBS name (Artificial Intelligence – Program Work) suggest enterprise IT/AI enablement rather than nuclear data digitization or acquisition-specific tooling, making the business and management platform challenge the best fit.</data>
      <data key="d18">BASELINE</data>
      <data key="d19">15148200.0</data>
      <data key="d20">NAIM</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="sub_office:naim:e600e447ad" target="mstc_challenge:accelerating_business_and_management_processes_f:1739dbeef6" id="funds_challenge_evidence:programming_record_programming_data_fynsp27_wide_csv_29">
      <data key="d11">funds_challenge_evidence</data>
      <data key="d12">1</data>
      <data key="d13">classification_pipeline</data>
      <data key="d14">29</data>
      <data key="d15">medium</data>
      <data key="d16">False</data>
      <data key="d17">The scope description focuses on AI-enabled cybersecurity tools that support the enterprise, which aligns best with improving and securing enterprise-wide business and management IT operations rather than directly targeting acquisition workflows or production/safety in nuclear facilities. The WBS and STAT both indicate Enterprise Operations and Information Technology &amp; Cybersecurity, suggesting cross-cutting enterprise support to underlying business processes, consistent with the business and management challenge. There is no explicit emphasis on acquisition lifecycle or production/safety basis activities, so the business and management processes challenge is the closest fit.</data>
      <data key="d18">BASELINE</data>
      <data key="d19">353798381.0</data>
      <data key="d20">NAIM</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="sub_office:naim:e600e447ad" target="mstc_challenge:streamlining_production_removing_red_tape_and_en:5c22e0a6a3" id="funds_challenge_evidence:programming_record_programming_data_csv_57">
      <data key="d11">funds_challenge_evidence</data>
      <data key="d12">1</data>
      <data key="d13">classification_pipeline</data>
      <data key="d14">57</data>
      <data key="d15">medium</data>
      <data key="d16">False</data>
      <data key="d17">The scope description focuses on “AI-enabled cybersecurity tools that support the enterprise,” which aligns best with cross-cutting enterprise business/IT enablement rather than mission-specific acquisition or production/safety workflows. The STAT and WBS context (“Information Technology &amp; Cybersecurity,” “Enterprise Operations”) and the AI GENESIS PLATFORM interest area indicate foundational enterprise platforms and tools that underpin business and management processes across the organization. Among the options, this most closely matches the challenge centered on accelerating business and management processes for national security missions, even though the description is brief and does not explicitly mention PPBE or HR, hence medium confidence. SME correction: Corrected</data>
      <data key="d18">BASELINE</data>
      <data key="d19">467378982.0</data>
      <data key="d20">NAIM</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="sub_office:naim:e600e447ad" target="mstc_challenge:streamlining_production_removing_red_tape_and_en:5c22e0a6a3" id="funds_challenge_evidence:programming_record_programming_data_csv_61">
      <data key="d11">funds_challenge_evidence</data>
      <data key="d12">1</data>
      <data key="d13">classification_pipeline</data>
      <data key="d14">61</data>
      <data key="d15">medium</data>
      <data key="d16">False</data>
      <data key="d17">The scope emphasizes enterprise AI enablement: upgrading core computing, enhancing enterprise connectivity, deploying secure AI models and tools, strengthening data governance and cybersecurity, and AI workforce training as foundational for current operations and future Genesis AI initiatives. Among the options, this most closely aligns with the acquisition-focused challenge, which explicitly depends on an AI-enabled acquisition ecosystem, secure data platforms, and enterprise AI tooling. The other two challenges are more narrowly focused on design/production integration and historic nuclear data digitization, which are not mentioned in the scope description. SME correction: Corrected</data>
      <data key="d18">UFR</data>
      <data key="d19">636625000.0</data>
      <data key="d20">NAIM</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="sub_office:naim:e600e447ad" target="mstc_challenge:increasing_experimental_capacity_at_nuclear_rese:e9a4a1153b" id="funds_challenge_evidence:programming_record_programming_data_csv_60">
      <data key="d11">funds_challenge_evidence</data>
      <data key="d12">1</data>
      <data key="d13">classification_pipeline</data>
      <data key="d14">60</data>
      <data key="d15">low</data>
      <data key="d16">False</data>
      <data key="d17">The scope describes a mixed-classification high-performance computing resource to efficiently support NNSA’s growing mission demand, under an AI Genesis Platform interest area. It is a general-purpose, cross-mission HPC capability rather than clearly tied to business/management workflows or to design–production integration for deterrence. Among the three options, it most plausibly underpins mission operations such as nuclear threat assessment and response that require mixed-classification HPC and AI, but the description is generic, so confidence is low. SME correction: Corrected</data>
      <data key="d18">UFR</data>
      <data key="d19">45000000.0</data>
      <data key="d20">NAIM</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="sub_office:naim:e600e447ad" target="mstc_challenge:accelerating_nuclear_threat_assessment_preparedn:b962e35516" id="funds_challenge_evidence:programming_record_programming_data_fynsp27_wide_csv_58">
      <data key="d11">funds_challenge_evidence</data>
      <data key="d12">1</data>
      <data key="d13">classification_pipeline</data>
      <data key="d14">58</data>
      <data key="d15">medium</data>
      <data key="d16">False</data>
      <data key="d17">The scope description emphasizes AI innovation that improves DOE fire response capabilities, increases AI capacity for mission-critical classified work, and benefits NNSA’s radiological and nuclear emergency response missions, along with deployment of AI infrastructure for NNSA users. Among the options, this aligns most directly with the challenge focused on accelerating nuclear threat assessment, preparedness, and response, which centers on AI-enabled fusion and decision support for radiological/nuclear emergency operations. The other challenges focus more narrowly on forensic attribution or experimental facility throughput, which are not indicated in the scope text. Overall AI infrastructure and inter-site connectivity are enabling capabilities, but the only mission-specific reference is to radiological and nuclear emergency response, pointing to the emergency response–oriented challenge.</data>
      <data key="d18">UFR</data>
      <data key="d19">362324875.0</data>
      <data key="d20">NAIM</data>
    </edge>
