      <data key="d15">low</data>
      <data key="d16">False</data>
      <data key="d17">The scope description is extremely terse: “NSM portion for Developing Artificial Intelligence Tools for Nuclear Deterrence.” It only clearly indicates AI applied to nuclear deterrence under an AI National Security Memorandum. All three candidate challenges relate to deterrence and AI, so the mapping relies on supporting context. The WBS/STAT hierarchy is “Assessment Science / Primary Assessment Technologies,” which aligns more naturally with nuclear material and radiological assessment/forensics than with design–production integration or broad emergency response fusion. Among the options, strengthening deterrence through attribution of nuclear and radiological signatures is the closest fit to primary assessment technologies, but the lack of explicit forensic or attribution language in the scope description limits certainty, so confidence is low.</data>
      <data key="d18">ROT</data>
      <data key="d19">25831000.0</data>
      <data key="d20">NA11</data>
    </edge>
