      <data key="d15">low</data>
      <data key="d16">False</data>
      <data key="d17">The scope description and program request only state "Developing Artificial Intelligence Tools for Nuclear Deterrence" with no further detail on whether the AI is for design/production integration, forensic attribution, or real-time threat assessment/response. The STAT/WBS context is "Assessment Science" and "Primary Assessment Technologies," which suggests tools that assess or characterize nuclear performance or materials rather than production workflows. Among the three challenges, both the attribution and threat-assessment options involve assessment, but the attribution challenge is more tightly tied to deterrence via clear nuclear signatures and forensic accountability, aligning somewhat with "nuclear deterrence" in the title. However, the evidence is very sparse and non-specific, so the mapping is uncertain.</data>
      <data key="d18">ROT</data>
      <data key="d19">11008000.0</data>
      <data key="d20">NA11</data>
    </edge>
