    <edge source="programming_record:programming_data_fynsp27_wide_csv:58" target="mstc_challenge:accelerating_nuclear_threat_assessment_preparedn:b962e35516" id="classified_as_challenge:programming_record_programming_data_fynsp27_wide_csv_58">
      <data key="d11">classified_as_challenge</data>
      <data key="d12">1</data>
      <data key="d13">classification_pipeline</data>
      <data key="d14">58</data>
      <data key="d15">medium</data>
      <data key="d16">False</data>
      <data key="d17">The scope description emphasizes AI innovation that improves DOE fire response capabilities, increases AI capacity for mission-critical classified work, and benefits NNSA’s radiological and nuclear emergency response missions, along with deployment of AI infrastructure for NNSA users. Among the options, this aligns most directly with the challenge focused on accelerating nuclear threat assessment, preparedness, and response, which centers on AI-enabled fusion and decision support for radiological/nuclear emergency operations. The other challenges focus more narrowly on forensic attribution or experimental facility throughput, which are not indicated in the scope text. Overall AI infrastructure and inter-site connectivity are enabling capabilities, but the only mission-specific reference is to radiological and nuclear emergency response, pointing to the emergency response–oriented challenge.</data>
      <data key="d18">UFR</data>
      <data key="d19">362324875.0</data>
      <data key="d20">NAIM</data>
    </edge>
