    <edge source="sub_office:na20:3f63a5531c" target="mstc_challenge:safeguarding_nuclear_materials_from_proliferatio:616a01e1a8" id="leads_challenge">
      <data key="d11">leads_challenge</data>
      <data key="d12">1</data>
      <data key="d13">ProgramOffice_Mapping.csv</data>
      <data key="d14">6</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="sub_office:na20:3f63a5531c" target="mstc_challenge:safeguarding_nuclear_materials_from_proliferatio:616a01e1a8" id="funds_challenge_evidence:programming_record_programming_data_csv_36">
      <data key="d11">funds_challenge_evidence</data>
      <data key="d12">1</data>
      <data key="d13">classification_pipeline</data>
      <data key="d14">36</data>
      <data key="d15">medium</data>
      <data key="d16">True</data>
      <data key="d17">The scope description is generic (“AI PROGRAM WORK”), so supporting fields must guide the mapping. The STAT hierarchy is Defense Nuclear Nonproliferation R&amp;D with a focus on Proliferation Detection, which aligns directly with the nonproliferation monitoring and anomaly detection focus of “Safeguarding Nuclear Materials from Proliferation Threats.” The other challenges center on AI-induced proliferation risk and on materials discovery/production, which do not match the proliferation detection mission indicated by the STAT and WBS context.</data>
      <data key="d18">BASELINE</data>
      <data key="d19">165000000.0</data>
      <data key="d20">NA20</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="sub_office:na20:3f63a5531c" target="mstc_challenge:safeguarding_nuclear_materials_from_proliferatio:616a01e1a8" id="funds_challenge_evidence:programming_record_programming_data_csv_37">
      <data key="d11">funds_challenge_evidence</data>
      <data key="d12">1</data>
      <data key="d13">classification_pipeline</data>
      <data key="d14">37</data>
      <data key="d15">high</data>
      <data key="d16">True</data>
      <data key="d17">The scope description focuses on accelerating AI for challenging proliferation detection scenarios, including AI-driven process modeling and system-of-systems digital twins, directly aligned with detecting and analyzing nuclear proliferation activities. This matches the Safeguarding Nuclear Materials from Proliferation Threats challenge, which emphasizes AI-enabled analysis of diverse data to detect proliferation-relevant anomalies. Supporting evidence includes the STAT lines for Proliferation Detection and the program request title “AI ACCELERATION FOR DETECTION OF FOREIGN PROLIFERATION,” both clearly oriented toward operational nonproliferation monitoring rather than archival data (Harnessing Historic Data) or AI policy/control concerns ((CUI) Preventing AI-Induced Nuclear Proliferation).</data>
      <data key="d18">UFR</data>
      <data key="d19">342000000.0</data>
      <data key="d20">NA20</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="sub_office:na20:3f63a5531c" target="mstc_challenge:safeguarding_nuclear_materials_from_proliferatio:616a01e1a8" id="funds_challenge_evidence:programming_record_programming_data_csv_39">
      <data key="d11">funds_challenge_evidence</data>
      <data key="d12">1</data>
      <data key="d13">classification_pipeline</data>
      <data key="d14">39</data>
      <data key="d15">high</data>
      <data key="d16">True</data>
      <data key="d17">The scope focuses on implementing activities under EO 14299 and expanding IAEA safeguards at U.S. nuclear facilities, which is directly tied to international safeguards, nonproliferation, and monitoring of nuclear materials. The WBS and STAT lines are in Nonproliferation and Arms Control / Civil Nuclear Cooperation &amp; Engagement, and the funding office is NA-20, all of which align with strengthening safeguards and detecting/protecting against proliferation threats rather than acquisition reform or experimental facility throughput.</data>
      <data key="d18">UFR</data>
      <data key="d19">20000000.0</data>
      <data key="d20">NA20</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="sub_office:na20:3f63a5531c" target="mstc_challenge:safeguarding_nuclear_materials_from_proliferatio:616a01e1a8" id="funds_challenge_evidence:programming_record_programming_data_csv_40">
      <data key="d11">funds_challenge_evidence</data>
      <data key="d12">1</data>
      <data key="d13">classification_pipeline</data>
      <data key="d14">40</data>
      <data key="d15">medium</data>
      <data key="d16">True</data>
      <data key="d17">The scope focuses on export control IT upgrades to accelerate technical reviews of export licenses for U.S. nuclear and dual-use commodities. This is directly tied to nonproliferation and control of nuclear-related materials and technologies, aligning with safeguarding against proliferation threats. Supporting evidence includes STAT and WBS labels (Nonproliferation and Arms Control, Nuclear Export Controls) and NA-20 context. While there is an element of process efficiency that could relate to acquisition, the core mission is export control for nonproliferation, making the safeguarding challenge the best fit.</data>
      <data key="d18">UFR</data>
      <data key="d19">8000000.0</data>
      <data key="d20">NA20</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="sub_office:na20:3f63a5531c" target="mstc_challenge:safeguarding_nuclear_materials_from_proliferatio:616a01e1a8" id="funds_challenge_evidence:programming_record_programming_data_fynsp27_wide_csv_30">
      <data key="d11">funds_challenge_evidence</data>
      <data key="d12">1</data>
      <data key="d13">classification_pipeline</data>
      <data key="d14">30</data>
      <data key="d15">high</data>
      <data key="d16">False</data>
      <data key="d17">The scope focuses on developing technologies to detect and characterize foreign nuclear weapons programs and to interdict nuclear materials outside regulatory control, which directly aligns with nonproliferation monitoring and threat detection. This matches the Safeguarding Nuclear Materials from Proliferation Threats challenge, which emphasizes AI-enabled detection of proliferation-relevant anomalies and enhanced monitoring of nuclear materials. The STAT lines (Defense Nuclear Nonproliferation R&amp;D, Proliferation Detection) and NA-20 context further support this mapping rather than acquisition reform or AI-induced proliferation policy concerns.</data>
      <data key="d18">BASELINE</data>
      <data key="d19">165000000.0</data>
      <data key="d20">NA20</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="sub_office:na20:3f63a5531c" target="mstc_challenge:strengthening_deterrence_through_attribution_of:12318fe125" id="partners_on_challenge">
      <data key="d11">partners_on_challenge</data>
